cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this diagnostic study, its code, or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "Does Feature Engineering Improve ATE Estimation in Double Machine Learning? A Controlled Diagnostic Study on Target Leakage"
abstract: >-
  Adding five domain-derived features plus a dispersion index to a Double Machine Learning
  pipeline reduced estimated ATE bias by 73.75% and collapsed the estimator's standard
  deviation by 4.00x. Under a controlled ablation holding the feature set fixed and toggling
  only the leakage channel, repeated over 30 independent random seeds, the improvement
  vanished completely (-0.65%, paired t = -0.137, p = 0.892; 16/30 wins). The gain was
  entirely attributable to target leakage: one derived feature was a deterministic function
  of the untreated potential outcome Y(0). The study characterises the empirical signature of
  leakage and distils the findings into a diagnostic checklist for practitioners.
